    "question": {
      "source_id": "unreconciled:step-q212208",
      "problem": "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $Z$ as follows: \n\n$\\,$ $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x-{y}^{2}=0$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-1,-1)\\notin R$ since the pair does not satisfy the underlying condition:\n\n$$\n(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-2\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- $(0,0)\\in R$ since the pair satisfies the underlying condition:\n\n$$\n(0)-(0)^{2}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(-1,-1)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(0,0)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
